<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglicanism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anglican Communion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anglicanism is the Christian tradition that grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> after its break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> in the 1530s. It combines Catholic church order with Protestant teaching, expressed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>. Spread by British settlement and missions, it now forms a worldwide communion of churches with tens of millions of members, many of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglicanism.jpg" alt="Anglicanism" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antony McCallum: Who is the uploader, photographer, full copyright owner and proprietor of WyrdLight.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury-cathedral-wyrdlight.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Anglicanism declared its independence from the Holy See through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-supremacy-1534">Act of Supremacy</a> in 1534, a separation consolidated by the Elizabethan Settlement of 1559. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> kept the apostolic succession of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, while its formularies of the mid-sixteenth century correspond closely to those of historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. The Settlement joined the radical 1552 prayer book to the conservative 1549 one in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> of 1559. In the first half of the seventeenth century some Anglican divines presented the church as a middle way, a via media, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, Anglican congregations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and British North America became autonomous churches with their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and Christian missions this model was adopted for many new churches, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Australasia and the Asia-Pacific. The term Anglicanism was coined in the late nineteenth century for the tradition common to these churches and the Scottish Episcopal Church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Anglicans base their faith on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, the traditions of the apostolic church, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a> and, historically, the Thirty-nine Articles. Doctrine is not set by a central authority or a founder, and high-church Anglicans look to the prayer books as its main expression, by the principle lex orandi, lex credendi, &quot;the law of prayer is the law of belief&quot;. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>, first issued in 1549, replaced the various <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rites with one volume in English. Its 1662 version was mandatory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and in standard use until the mid-twentieth century.</p><p>The ministry is threefold: deacons, priests and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. The Anglican Communion has no international juridical body. Its provinces have historically been in full communion with the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, whose archbishop is first among equals with no direct authority outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He invites the bishops to the Lambeth Conference, first convened in 1867 by Archbishop Charles Longley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford-movement">Oxford Movement</a> of the mid-nineteenth century revived practices close to Roman Catholicism, and the Tractarians, especially John Henry Newman, saw the English church as a via media between Protestant and Catholic traditions; Newman later rejected the theory. Anglicans have been active in social causes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a> for factory and mine reform, Frederick Denison Maurice in Christian socialism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Desmond Tutu</a> and Ted Scott against apartheid. By 2020 the centre of Anglicanism had shifted to the Global South, with 63,556,000 adherents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and 24,400,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglicanism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church of England, created by the Reformation and given its liturgy in Thomas Cranmer’s Book of Common Prayer, became the mother church of the worldwide <b>Anglican Communion</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…is sung or said at the main evening service, Vespers in the Catholic and Lutheran churches and Evening Prayer or Evensong in the <b>Anglican Communion</b>, where it is usually paired with the Nunc dimittis and followed by the Gloria Patri, a doxology not found in the original text.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Kasagama Kyebambe IV the court adopted <b>Anglicanism</b>, and the Toro Agreement of 1900 defined the kingdom's place in the Uganda Protectorate, with its capital at Fort Portal.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-toro">Kingdom of Toro</a></li></ul></section>
